en you  can just take it with you as you need  which three of these four power banks  can actually do the next one i named  kickstand since it's the only one with a  kickstand so you can prop it up while  it's attached and charging your phone  it's 34 bucks and it is thinner than big  gray but also taller so it ends up  having the biggest footprint overall and  since it sits flush with your phone it's  the only one that won't properly work  with the 12 mini kickstand has a second  trick that no other magnetic power bank  has and that is a lightning port in  addition to usb type-c which can be used  to charge the power bank but not your  phone it's rated at 5 000 milliamp hours  rating just like the next one the hyper  power and also the anchor it also has by  far the strongest magnet which is almost  too strong but unfortunately since the  bottom uses a really slick plastic  material it constantly twists on the  body which is super annoying to me since  it never really sits straight and with  the iphone 12 it rubs against the camera  bump causing plastic to literally be  shaved off the whole thing feels cheap  and the kickstand is flimsy so it seems  like it won't last very long and it will  end up breaking off the next one is the  only one that doesn't just get rebranded  and sold the hyper power it's 40 bucks  and definitely seems like a brand name  product based on the design and the  quality plastic that is used it is on  the smaller side but it's definitely  thick and the bottom is a softer plastic  but not rubbery hyperpower does have an  alignment magnet but it's not very  strong so it's easy to twist the power  bank and in general this one has the  weakest magnet overall so sometimes you  drop it in your pocket and it will  actually detach from your phone and stop  charging which the other ones don't do  and our last one is blue mini which is  by far the smallest one being just as  thin as kickstand but with a tiny  footprint which works perfectly for the  iphone 12 mini not only that but it's  the cheapest one as well at only 27 and  the only one with a fully really grippy  rubber back so it is very resistant to  twisting and even though it's by far the  smallest one out of the group its magnet  is actually the second strongest which  was surprising now i was also surprised  about the battery rating which is just  slightly less than the previous two at 4  200 milliamp hours but we'll see if it's  actually that high  and it's also the only one that can't  pass through wireless charging along  with that the blue mini accepts the most  actual power when charging at 16.7 watts  and because the battery is the smallest  it will charge very quickly big gray and  kickstand both accept 16.4 watts so just  slightly less but of course kickstand  can also accept power through the  lightning port which maxes out at 9.7  real world watts so don't use that if  you want to charge it fast the slowest  max 8 power bank is the only one with  the brand name hyper at 10 real world  watts through usb type c so we'll take  the longest to charge up and with that  if you want to charge an iphone from the  usb type c port it is also the slowest  at only 8 watts for some reason the rest  of the power banks output 10.4 watts to  an iphone and the only one with the usb  type-a ports can pass through 4.8 watts  that way now one thing that really gets  to me is a ton of these brands advertise  up to 15 watts of wireless charging  through magsafe well that's simply not  true because an iphone can only accept  7.5 watts if you're not using the  official max safe charger so i decided  to test what they actually get through  its magnetic coils let's start out with  the most disappointing one the blue mini  even though it accepts the most power  through usb type c for charging it only  outputs about three and a half watts  after efficiency loss which means it's  most likely based on 5 watts for  wireless charging and that's also what  anker's is rated big ray showed 6 watts  which is better and hyper and kickstand  showed 7 watts which is likely based on  7.5 watt qi charging now of course  wattage can vary as you go on so let's  take a look at what is actually our  charging time and how much each phone  gets charged with these power banks for  all of my testing i use an iphone 12  which is by far the most popular new  iphone but the 12 pro should charge  exactly the same since it has the same  battery and i'll also comment on the 12  mini and 12 pro max in just a bit i made  sure that the iphone 12 was completely  drained and attached each battery to see  how long they would take to turn on your  phone which is always such a frustrating  weight big grade took 4 minutes and 20  seconds to start the phone kickstand  took a much longer 6 minutes hyper was  the quickest at only 3 minutes and 15  seconds and blue mini took 5 minutes and  30 seconds after 15 minutes blue mini  only hit six percent charge which was  disappointing after 15 minutes of  waiting big gray managed nine percent  which is right in line with my desktop  qii chargers and surprisingly hyper and  kickstand charged at double blue mini's  rate at 12 after 15 minutes at the 30  minute mark all chargers about double  their charge rate with the blue mini  hitting 13  which the other two pretty much hit in  the first 15. let's skip 45 minutes and  jump right to the one hour mark hyper  and kickstand both reach the same 42  percent which isn't impressive but it's  still faster than my desktop g chargers  which reached the same speed at big  great at 35 percent and blue mini is  slightly behind at 28  and jumping to the one and a half hour  mark which is where wired fast charging  would have fully charged the iphone 12  our two faster chargers hit 60 percent  big ray hit 53 and blue mini a sad 42  and after two to two and a half hours  some chargers started to drastically  slow down while others like the blue  mini and hyper completely ran out of  power  blue mini ended up only charging the  iphone 12 to 68  whereas hyper reached 85 in the same  time kickstand slowed but kept going for  another half hour where it died after  reaching 95 percent and big gray kind of  stalled between two and a half to three  hours staying at around 80 percent even  though optimized charging was turned off  and then it sped up slightly and kept  going until the four hour mark where it  fully charged our iphone 12 being the  only one to hit 100  but not only that it was still showing  charge so i drained my iphone 12 for the  fifth time and believe it or not it  ended up charging it another 50  where the others couldn't even do 100  once now the question is does it  actually have ten thousand milliamp  hours of capacity like the listing well  i'm not so sure especially since the  listing also shows somewhere around  seven thousand milliamp hours as a side  note at the bottom so i went ahead and i  took the percentage of charge from each  power bank and i divided that  based on the milliamp hours that the  iphone 12's battery is listed at and  that gave me a real-world charge of 4  200 milliamp hours once we account for  loss due to heat and other factors  kickstand provided 2675 in hyper 2400  and blue mini only 1915 milliamp hours  meaning it wouldn't even fully charge an  iphone 12 mini so based on the test  results you would think that big rate is  probably the one to buy since it can  actually charge your phone all the way  it has pass-through charging and  multiple outputs as well and has a  decent magnetic connection and you may  be right but for me it is my number two  choice that is because it is definitely  by far the heaviest and chunkiest one  and it's also fairly slow so if you're  planning to charge your phone while  using it it's not going to be very  comfortable for long and after a while  your hand is just going to get tired and  heavy so personally i'm not really a fan  of that since it's just way too heavy to  use this way and even though the blue  mini has the least amount of features  and it's by far the slowest it is  actually my favorite because of how  small it is and how lightweight it is i  can easily keep it on my phone while  using it as it doesn't add much bulk and  it doesn't slip off so i use it to keep  my battery from draining and it slowly  tops it off as well now personally i'm  not a fan of the other two even though  they charge fast because they come off  way too easily and they're not very  comfortable while using it and with that  they also don't give you a full charge  and the same thing goes for anchor's  battery bank based on all the reviews  that i read but you guys let me know  what you think down below in the comment  section which charger would fit your  personal needs better and why i will  have all the link down below in the  description for you guys to check out  the prices at the time that you're  watching the video and if you guys  enjoyed it go ahead and click that  circle above to subscribe and check out  one of the other great videos right over  there this has been max and i'll see you  in the next video  [Music]  you

